---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PlateHeader from '../components/PlateHeader.astro';
import ResumeGameApp from '../components/ResumeGame';
import ErrorBoundary from '../components/shared/ErrorBoundary';
import GuideCoach from '../components/shared/GuideCoach';

const resumeSchemas = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'How does the resume scoring work?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'The Resume Game scores each line out of 100 for four parts it shows you: what you did, what the work was, what happened because of it, and a number that says what it counts. It then asks questions about the parts that are missing.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Can I upload my existing resume?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Yes. You can upload .txt, .md, .docx, or .pdf files, or simply paste your resume text directly into the editor.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Is my resume data private?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Yes. All text is processed in your browser. No data is sent to any server.',
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];

const resumeNoScriptContext = [
  'Resume Bullet Game is the proof-building tool in 5 Whys Career Studio. It focuses on one practical problem: many resume bullets describe responsibility without showing evidence. The interactive version scores each line for four parts: what you did, what the work was, what happened because of it, and a number that says what it counts. The point is to make the useful work easy for a hiring manager or interviewer to understand. A loud bullet does not help if nobody can tell what you did.',
  'Without JavaScript, you can still use the same review pattern manually. Take one bullet at a time and check whether it names the action, the object of the work, the constraint or audience, and the result. Stronger bullets usually include a specific verb, a system or process, a scale marker, and an outcome. Weak bullets often hide behind words like helped, worked on, responsible for, supported, assisted, or managed without explaining what changed.',
  'A practical rewrite flow is simple: paste or write the existing bullet, underline the real contribution, say where or for whom, start with the word for what you actually did, and make the outcome visible if it is known. If a number would be fake, say what changed instead: shorter waits, fewer mistakes, a schedule that held, or a process other people started using.',
  'Resume text can include employers, clients, systems, metrics, and personal history. That is why the tool keeps everything in the browser during normal use. Exported files are controlled by whatever software you open them in after leaving the page. Review every generated or rewritten bullet before sending it anywhere, and keep the final voice direct, specific, and honest.',
];
---

<Base
  title="The Resume Game"
  description="Score each resume line for what you did and what happened because of it, then rewrite the weak ones in your own words."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'The Resume Game', href: '/resume-game/' },
  ]}
  structuredData={resumeSchemas}
  noScriptContext={resumeNoScriptContext}
>
  <div class="relative space-y-10 overflow-hidden px-4 pb-16 pt-4 sm:pt-6">
    <PlateHeader
      tag="Plate II · Prove"
      roman="II"
      title="The Resume Game"
      description="Paste a resume and every achievement line is scored for action, result, and measure. Rewrite the weak ones and watch the score move."
      accent="gold"
    />
    <GuideCoach client:load tool="resume-game" />
    <section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl">
      <ErrorBoundary client:load>
        <ResumeGameApp
          client:load
          showHeader={false}
          className="px-0 pb-8 pt-0 overflow-hidden"
        />
      </ErrorBoundary>
    </section>

    <section class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl" aria-labelledby="ats-heading">
      <div class="grid gap-x-14 gap-y-6 border-t border-border/40 pt-8 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr]">
        <div class="space-y-3">
          <span class="eyebrow" style="color: hsl(var(--gold))">A question everyone asks</span>
          <h2
            id="ats-heading"
            class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-3xl"
          >
            What an applicant tracking system reads
          </h2>
          <p class="max-w-md text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
            Most employers file applications in software before a person reads them. There is no
            universal score, and no site can tell you how a given employer's system will rank you.
            These are the things that are true of nearly all of them.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="max-w-[68ch] border-t border-border/40 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          {
            [
              [
                'It reads text, in the order the file stores it.',
                'Two columns, tables and text boxes can come out scrambled or not at all. One column is safest. Upload your file above to see the order yours is read in.',
              ],
              [
                'It looks for ordinary headings.',
                'Education, Experience, Skills, Projects. A clever heading may not be recognized as a section.',
              ],
              [
                'It may miss what is in the header or footer.',
                'Keep your name, email and phone in the body of the page.',
              ],
              [
                'A person searches it with the posting\'s words.',
                'Where the posting\'s wording is true of you, use it. Do not paste in words that are not.',
              ],
              [
                'Send a Word file or a text-based PDF.',
                'A scan or a photo of a resume has no text to read.',
              ],
            ].map(([point, detail]) => (
              <li class="border-b border-border/40 py-3">
                <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">{point}</span>
                <span class="block text-muted-foreground">{detail}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      class="relative mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl reveal"
      aria-labelledby="resume-guide-heading"
    >
      <div class="mb-8 border-t border-border/40 pt-8">
        <span class="eyebrow" style="color: hsl(var(--gold))">How it works</span>
        <h2
          id="resume-guide-heading"
          class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-3xl"
        >
          From what you did to what it shows
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-[1.1fr_0.9fr] reveal-stagger">
        <article
          class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card/50 p-6 transition-colors duration-200 hover:border-gold/40 hover:bg-card/70"
        >
          <div
            class="mb-4 flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-gold/15"
          >
            <svg
              aria-hidden="true"
              class="h-5 w-5 text-gold"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.8"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M19.5 14.25v-2.625a3.375 3.375 0 00-3.375-3.375h-1.5A1.125 1.125 0 0113.5 7.125v-1.5a3.375 3.375 0 00-3.375-3.375H8.25m0 12.75h7.5m-7.5 3H12M10.5 2.25H5.625c-.621 0-1.125.504-1.125 1.125v17.25c0 .621.504 1.125 1.125 1.125h12.75c.621 0 1.125-.504 1.125-1.125V11.25a9 9 0 00-9-9z"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">When to use it</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
            Use the Resume Game when a line says you were busy but not what came of it. Paste your
            resume, or a few lines, and rewrite one at a time until a reader can see what you did.
            It works for a first draft, a last look, and a quick check before you apply.
          </p>
          <div
            class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-gold/20 bg-gold/6 p-3"
          >
            <p class="eyebrow" style="color: hsl(var(--gold))">Tip</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
              Start with the line you are least sure of. That is usually where a small change shows most.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article
          class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card/50 p-6 transition-colors duration-200 hover:border-gold/40 hover:bg-card/70"
        >
          <div
            class="mb-4 flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-gold/15"
          >
            <svg
              aria-hidden="true"
              class="h-5 w-5 text-gold"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.8"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M10.5 6a7.5 7.5 0 107.5 7.5h-7.5V6z"></path>
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M13.5 10.5H21A7.5 7.5 0 0013.5 3v7.5z"></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">What the score means</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
            The score tells you which line to rewrite next. It does not predict whether you get the
            job. Strong bullets usually name the work, show scale, explain the result, and avoid
            vague verbs, so a lower score points at the part that is still missing instead of
            leaving you to guess.
          </p>
          <div
            class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-gold/20 bg-gold/6 p-3"
          >
            <p class="eyebrow" style="color: hsl(var(--gold))">Tip</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
              A line with no number can still score strong if it says what happened because of
              the work.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article
          class="rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card/50 p-6 md:col-span-2 transition-colors duration-200 hover:border-gold/40 hover:bg-card/70"
        >
          <div
            class="mb-4 flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-gold/15"
          >
            <svg
              aria-hidden="true"
              class="h-5 w-5 text-gold"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.8"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M16.862 4.487l1.687-1.688a1.875 1.875 0 112.652 2.652L10.582 16.07a4.5 4.5 0 01-1.897 1.13L6 18l.8-2.685a4.5 4.5 0 011.13-1.897l8.932-8.931zm0 0L19.5 7.125M18 14v4.75A2.25 2.25 0 0115.75 21H5.25A2.25 2.25 0 013 18.75V8.25A2.25 2.25 0 015.25 6H10"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">How to revise</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
            Rewrite one part at a time. Start with what you did, say what the work was, then what
            happened because of it, and add a number only if you have a true one. The rewrite
            should still sound like your work. It should just be easier to check.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
            No number? Say what changed instead: what got faster or easier, what stopped going
            wrong, or who used what you made. Plain, specific words read better than big ones.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
            Save the strongest rewrite, then compare it against the role before sending.
          </p>
          <div
            class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-gold/20 bg-gold/6 p-3"
          >
            <p class="eyebrow" style="color: hsl(var(--gold))">Tip</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
              Read the bullet out loud. If it sounds like a job description, it is probably too
              vague.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
